@pixel_tern13: Good thread on evals, but the recent replies are drifting into JSON tooling. Quick take on LLM capability evals first: pin down the exact behaviour you want, write a small golden set, and separate "did it succeed" from "how good was it" so you can track both. Keep a held-out split you never tune against.

On cost: run cheap automated graders first and save the expensive LLM-as-judge pass for the subset that needs it. Watch latency too, since eval suites that take hours quietly kill iteration speed.

Also, before trusting any "60% faster" claim, benchmark it on your own payloads.

